By the end of the 6th century BC, Rome and many of its Italian neighbours entered a period of turbulence. Archaeological evidence implies some degree of large-scale warfare.{{Sfnp|Boatwright et al.|2012|p=43}} According to tradition and later writers such as [[Livy]], the [[Roman Republic]] was established {{Circa|509&nbsp;BC}},{{Sfnp|Cornell|1995|pp=[https://archive.org/details/beginningsofrome0000corn/page/215/mode/1up 215 ff.]}} when the last of the seven kings of Rome, [[Tarquin the Proud]], was [[Overthrow of the Roman monarchy|deposed]] and a system based on annually elected [[Roman Magistrate|magistrates]] and various representative assemblies was established.{{Sfnp|Matyszak|2003|pp=43–4}} A [[constitution of the Roman Republic|constitution]] set a series of checks and balances, and a [[separation of powers]]. The most important magistrates were the two [[Roman consul|consuls]], who together exercised executive authority such as {{lang|la|[[imperium]]}}, or military command.{{Sfnp|Adkins|Adkins|1998|pp=[https://books.google.ca/books?id=9JJdqJ8YGH8C&pg=PA41 41–2]}} The consuls had to work with the [[Roman Senate|Senate]], which was initially an advisory council of the ranking nobility, or [[patricians]], but grew in size and power.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hooker |first=Richard |date=1999 |title=Rome: The Roman Republic |website=[[Washington State University]] |url=http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/REPUBLIC.HTM |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514025151/http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/REPUBLIC.HTM |archive-date=14 May 2011 }}</ref>

Other magistrates of the Republic include [[tribune]]s, [[quaestor]]s, [[aedile]]s, [[praetor]]s and [[Roman censor|censors]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Long |first=George |author-link=George Long (scholar) |date=1878 |chapter=Magistratus  |editor-last=Smith |editor-first=William |editor-link=William Smith (lexicographer) |title=A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities |title-link=A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities |publisher=[[John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]] |pages=[https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofgree04smit/page/607/mode/1up 607-8] }} See also online text at [https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Magistratus.html penelope.uchicago.edu].</ref> The magistracies were originally restricted to [[patricians]], but were later opened to common people, or [[plebeians]].{{sfnp|Livy|1998|loc=Book Two, 33 (p. [https://archive.org/details/riseofromebookso00livy/page/104/mode/1up?q=sacrosanct 104])}} Republican voting assemblies included the {{lang|la|[[comitia centuriata]]}} ('centuriate assembly'), which voted on matters of war and peace and elected men to the most important offices, and the {{lang|la|[[comitia tributa]]}} ('tribal assembly'), which elected less important offices.{{Sfnp|Adkins|Adkins|1998|p=[https://books.google.ca/books?id=9JJdqJ8YGH8C&pg=PA39 39]}}

In the 4th century BC, Rome had come under attack by the [[Gauls]], who now extended their power in the Italian peninsula beyond the [[Po Valley]] and through Etruria. On 16 July 390&nbsp;BC, a Gallic army under the leadership of tribal chieftain [[Brennus (4th century BC)|Brennus]], defeated the Romans at the [[Battle of the Allia]] and marched to Rome. The Gauls looted and burned the city, then laid siege to the Capitoline Hill, where some Romans had barricaded themselves, for seven months. The Gauls then agreed to give the Romans peace in exchange for 1000 pounds of gold.{{efn|These are literally Roman {{lang|la|librae}}, from which the pound is derived.}} According to later legend, the Roman supervising the weighing noticed that the Gauls were using false scales. The Romans then took up arms and defeated the Gauls. Their victorious general [[Marcus Furius Camillus|Camillus]] remarked "With iron, not with gold, Rome buys her freedom."{{sfnp|Plutarch's Lives||loc=''Life of Camillus'', [https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html#29 29.2]}}

The Romans [[Roman conquest of Italy|gradually subdued]] the other peoples on the Italian peninsula, including the [[Etruscans]].{{r|n=Haywood1971_3508|r={{harvp|Haywood|1971|pp=[https://archive.org/details/ancientworld0000unse/page/350/mode/1up?q=Palestrina 350–8]}}.}} The last threat to Roman [[hegemony]] in Italy came when [[Taranto|Tarentum]], a major [[Ancient Greece|Greek]] colony, enlisted the aid of [[Pyrrhus of Epirus]] in 281&nbsp;BC, but this effort failed as well.<ref>{{cite web |title=Livy on Pyrrhus |website=Livius.org |translator=Jona Lendering |date=2020 |url=https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-on-pyrrhus/ }}</ref>{{r|Haywood1971_3508}} The Romans secured their conquests by founding [[Colonies in antiquity|Roman colonies]] in strategic areas, thereby establishing stable control over the region.{{r|Haywood1971_3508}}

==== Punic Wars ====
{{Main|Punic Wars}}
{{See also|Roman conquest of the Iberian Peninsula}}
[[File:Domain changes during the Punic Wars.gif|thumb|upright=1.8|Territorial changes over the course of the [[Punic Wars]]:
{{Legend|#b4d5b1|Roman possessions and close allies}}
{{Legend|#ffcb90|[[Carthaginian Empire]] and close allies }}]]
[[File:RUSSELL(1854) p182 Siege of Numantia.jpg|thumb|right|upright=0.9|The Roman siege of the [[Celtiberians|Celtiberian]] stronghold of [[Numantia]] in Spain in 133 BC<ref>{{Cite book |editor-last=Newark |editor-first=Tim |date=2016 |orig-date=2009 |title=The History of Warfare: The Ultimate Visual Guide to the History of Warfare from the Ancient World to the American Civil War |publisher=[[The Quarto Group#Imprints|Chartwell Books]] |page=[https://books.google.ca/books?id=27KTDgAAQBAJ&pg=PA57 57] |isbn=9780785834618 }}</ref>]]

In the 3rd century&nbsp;BC Rome faced a new and formidable opponent: [[Ancient Carthage|Carthage]], the other major power in the Western Mediterranean.{{sfnmp|1a1=Goldsworthy|1y=2006|1pp=25–6|2a1=Miles|2y=2010|2pp=[https://archive.org/details/carthagemustbede0000mile/page/174/mode/1up 174–6]}} The [[First Punic War]] began in 264&nbsp;BC, when the city of [[Messana]] asked for Carthage's help in their conflicts with [[Hiero II of Syracuse]]. After the Carthaginian intercession, Messana asked Rome to expel the Carthaginians. Rome entered this war because [[Syracuse, Sicily|Syracuse]] and Messana were too close to the newly conquered Greek cities of Southern Italy and Carthage was now able to make an offensive through Roman territory; along with this, Rome could extend its domain over [[Sicily]].{{sfnp|Dio's Roman History||loc=Vol. 1, [https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/11*.html#43 Fragments of Book 11, 43.1-6] (pp. 380-3)}}

Carthage was a maritime power, and the Roman lack of ships and naval experience made the path to the victory a long and difficult one for the [[Roman Republic]]. Despite this, after more than 20 years of war, Rome defeated Carthage and a peace treaty was signed. Among the reasons for the [[Second Punic War]]<ref>{{Cite book |last=Labberton |first=Robert H. |date=1886 |title=New historical atlas and general history |location=[[New York City]] |publisher=Townsend Mac Coun |page=[https://archive.org/details/newhistoricalatl00labb/page/35/mode/1up 35] }}</ref> was the subsequent war reparations Carthage acquiesced to at the end of the First Punic War.<ref>{{Cite EB1911 |last=Caspari |first=Maximilian Otto Bismarck |wstitle=Punic Wars |volume=22 |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |p=850}}</ref> The war began with the audacious invasion of Hispania by [[Hannibal]], who marched through [[Hispania]] to the Italian [[Alps]]. Hannibal's invasion lasted over 16 years, ravaging Italy, but ultimately Carthage was defeated in the decisive [[Battle of Zama]] in October 202&nbsp;BC.

More than a half century after these events, Carthage was left humiliated and the Republic's focus was now directed towards the [[Hellenistic]] kingdoms of Greece and [[Celtiberian Wars|revolts in Hispania]]. However, Carthage, having paid the war indemnity, felt that its commitments and submission to Rome had ceased, a vision not shared by the [[Roman Senate]]. The [[Third Punic War]] began when Rome declared war against Carthage in 149&nbsp;BC. Carthage resisted well at the first strike but could not withstand the attack of [[Scipio Aemilianus]], who entirely destroyed the city, enslaved all the citizens and gained control of that region, which became the province of [[Africa (Roman province)|Africa]]. All these wars resulted in Rome's first overseas conquests (Sicily, Hispania and Africa) and the rise of Rome as a significant imperial power.{{r|n=Haywood1971_37693|r={{Harvp|Haywood|1971|pp=[https://archive.org/details/ancientworld0000unse/page/376/mode/1up?q=chastised 376–93]}}.}}<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hooker |first=Richard |date=1999 |title=Rome: The Punic Wars |website=[[Washington State University]] |url=http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/PUNICWAR.HTM |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110104064031/http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/PUNICWARS.HTM |archive-date=4 January 2011 |access-date=9 May 2026 }}</ref>

=== Late Republic ===
After defeating the [[Macedon]]ian and [[Seleucid Empire]]s in the 2nd century BC, the [[Roman people|Romans]] became the dominant people of the [[Mediterranean Sea]].{{sfnp|Bury|1923|}}{{page needed|date=May 2026}}<ref>{{cite web |last=Hooker |first=Richard |date=1999 |title=Rome: The Conquest of the Hellenistic Empires |website=[[Washington State University]] |url=http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/CONQHELL.HTM  |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110501115720/http://www.wsu.edu/~dee/ROME/CONQHELL.HTM |archive-date=1 May 2011 |access-date=9 May 2026 }}</ref> The conquest of the Hellenistic kingdoms brought the Roman and Greek cultures in closer contact and the Roman elite, once rural, became cosmopolitan.

